//? Title: Needleman–Wunsch global alignment
//? Subtitle: Actual dynamic-programming cells, tied moves and end-to-end traceback
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Find a highest-scoring end-to-end alignment of two byte sequences under linear
match, mismatch and gap scores. We inspect the actual native score table and
traceback, retain the original AAAC/AGC example and three additional sequence
pairs, and compare full-table storage with the existing linear-space Hirschberg
implementation.
)

\toc

> Prefix optimality and fully penalized ends

Let H(i,j) be the best score aligning the first i bytes of A with the first j
bytes of B. The last column either consumes both bytes, only A, or only B. Those
exhaustive alternatives give
H(i,j)=max(H(i−1,j−1)+pair,H(i−1,j)+gap,H(i,j−1)+gap). Borders are H(i,0)=i·gap
and H(0,j)=j·gap: terminal gaps are charged, unlike a local or free-end
alignment. Induction on i+j proves the optimum. The returned traceback prefers
diagonal, then up, then left whenever scores tie; it selects one optimum, not
every optimal alignment.

> A tied cell in a tiny actual run

For AA versus A with scores1,−1,−1, the final diagonal and up candidates both
score zero. The native producer records its diagonal-first choice. The diagram
below reads its actual candidates; no second recurrence is implemented in the
report.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nw_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::needleman_wunsch_trace("AA", "A", {1, -1, -1});
    auto c = t.cells.back();
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("diag", {0, 2}, std::format("diagonal candidate {}", c.diagonal));
    d.node("up", {0, 0}, std::format("up candidate {}", c.up));
    d.node("left", {0, -2}, std::format("left candidate {}", c.left));
    d.node("chosen", {7, 0},
           std::format("H({}, {}) = {}\nactual {}", c.i, c.j, c.score,
                       dm::algorithms::needleman_wunsch_move_name(c.chosen)));
    for (auto id : {"diag", "up", "left"})
        d.edge(id, "chosen");
    std::print("Actual aligned rows: `{}` / `{}`, score {}.\n",
               t.run.alignment.a_aligned, t.run.alignment.b_aligned,
               t.run.alignment.score);
    d.save_svg("build/nw_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Actual aligned rows: `AA` / `-A`, score 0.
\image(figures/nw_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual candidates and first-maximum choice for AA versus A.)
\alttext(Diagonal and up tie at zero; the actual producer selects diagonal.)

> Retain the original AAAC versus AGC experiment

The original scoring is match1, mismatch−1 and gap−1. The native optimum is
zero, with aligned rows AAAC and -AGC under the stated tie policy. Every
displayed number below comes from the actual canonical table, including both
boundary prefixes. The plotted path follows the actual reverse traceback from
bottom right to origin; its axes are prefix lengths rather than a geographic
distance.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nw_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::needleman_wunsch_trace("AAAC", "AGC", {1, -1, -1});
    std::print("| A prefix length | empty B | A | AG | AGC |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.run.scores.size(); ++i) {
        std::print("| {} |", i);
        for (auto v : t.run.scores[i])
            std::print(" {} |", v);
        std::print("\n");
    }
    std::vector<double> x{double(t.b.size())}, y{double(t.a.size())};
    for (auto &s : t.traceback) {
        x.push_back(s.j_after);
        y.push_back(s.i_after);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::plot(x, y, "l", "actual traceback");
    p.points(x, y, "visited prefixes", {220, 38, 38}, 5).title(
        "Original AAAC / AGC: actual reverse traceback").x_label(
        "AGC prefix length").y_label("AAAC prefix length").save_svg(
        "build/nw_original.svg");
    std::print("\nActual score {}, rows `{}` / `{}`; {} allocated cells, {} actual recurrence cells, {} " "traceback columns.\n",
               t.run.alignment.score, t.run.alignment.a_aligned,
               t.run.alignment.b_aligned, t.run.cells, t.run.interior_cells,
               t.run.traceback_steps);
}
```
