> Storage, empty inputs and scoring controls

The full algorithm allocates (n+1)(m+1) score values and evaluates nm interior
recurrences, then emits at most n+m alignment columns. It therefore uses
O((n+1)(m+1)) work and table storage. The optional complete cell limit defaults
to1000000 and includes the boundary row and column. Ordinary needleman_wunsch
disables optional policies. The owned trace additionally counts3 scoring
scalars, n+m input bytes, n+1 row records, all score values, nm fill records and
three units per emitted column. A zero policy disables its limit; an
insufficient limit throws rather than returning a truncated history.

Hirschberg instead recomputes rolling rows and splits. Its recorded
peak_score_slots measures simultaneously live score-vector elements, excluding
outputs and recursion frames; this is compared with full-table values rather
than claiming total resident bytes. Scores must agree, although tied aligned
rows can differ with split orientation. Empty inputs produce penalized gaps.
Positive gap rewards are accepted and can make opposing inserted gaps optimal,
demonstrating why the scoring model matters.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nw_storage.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, full, rolling;
    std::print("| repeated input length | full score slots | Hirschberg peak score slots | scores agree " "|\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (int n : {0, 1, 4, 16, 64}) {
        std::string a(n, 'A'), b(n, 'A');
        auto r = dm::algorithms::needleman_wunsch_run(a, b);
        auto h = dm::algorithms::hirschberg_run(a, b);
        x.push_back(n);
        full.push_back(r.cells);
        rolling.push_back(h.work.peak_score_slots);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, r.cells,
                   h.work.peak_score_slots,
                   r.alignment.score == h.alignment.score);
    }
    auto p =
        dm::graphics::RPlot::plot(x, full, "l", "full table", {192, 59, 50});
    p.lines(x, rolling, "Hirschberg rows", {36, 105, 172}).title(
        "Actual score storage: full table versus rolling rows").x_label(
        "equal input lengths").y_label("actual score values").save_svg(
        "build/nw_storage.svg");
    auto r = dm::algorithms::needleman_wunsch("A", "A", {1, -1, 2});
    std::print("\nPositive-gap control: `{}` / `{}`, score {}.\n", r.a_aligned,
               r.b_aligned, r.score);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| repeated input length | full score slots | Hirschberg peak score slots | scores agree |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| 0 | 1 | 0 | true |
| 1 | 4 | 0 | true |
| 4 | 25 | 15 | true |
| 16 | 289 | 51 | true |
| 64 | 4225 | 195 | true |

Positive-gap control: `-A` / `A-`, score 4.
